{"cuisine/shaanxi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/shaanxi-cuisine","n":"Shaanxi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Shaanxi cuisine, or Qin cuisine, is derived from the native cooking styles of Shaanxi Province and parts of northwestern China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"shaanxi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaanxi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Xi'an Famous Foods: The Cuisine of Western China, from New York's Favorite Noodle Shop","ref":"cookbook/xi-an-famous-foods-the-cuisine-of-western-china-from-new-york-s-favorite-noodle-shop","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jason Wang with Jessica Chou, 2020","note":"Shaanxi-style noodles and dishes from the family behind the New York restaurant chain.","url":"https://abramsbooks.com/product/xian-famous-foods_9781647000080","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Guokui","dish/guokui"],["Liangpi","dish/liangpi"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired","n":"Singaporean Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Singapore","intro":[["Singaporean cuisine is derived from several ethnic groups in Singapore and has developed through centuries of political, economic, and social changes in the cosmopolitan city-state."],["Influences include the cuisines of the Malays/Indonesians, Chinese and the Indians as well as, Peranakan and Western traditions (particularly English and Portuguese-influenced Eurasian, known as Kristang). The cuisine has a medium spiciness range, mostly due to historical immigrant influence from South Asia and the Malay Archipelago. More modern influences from neighbouring regions such as Japan, Korea and Thailand are also present."],["In Singapore, food is viewed as crucial to its national identity and a unifying cultural thread. Singaporean literature declares eating a national pastime and food a national obsession. Food is a frequent topic of conversation among Singaporeans. Religious dietary strictures do exist; Muslims do not eat pork, Hindus and Buddhists do not eat beef. There is also a significant group of vegetarians/vegans, who are mainly Buddhist or Hindu. People from different communities often eat together, while being mindful of each other's culture and choosing food that is acceptable for all."],["In addition to venues serving traditional Singaporean food, restaurants serving cuisine from a diverse range of countries worldwide are also common in Singapore."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singaporean_cuisine#Chinese-inspired"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"],["Satay","dish/satay"],["Thali","dish/thali"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Bird","ingredient/bird"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Chilli pepper","ingredient/chilli-pepper"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Fennel","ingredient/fennel"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fried rice","ingredient/fried-rice"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/diet-in-sikhism":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/diet-in-sikhism","n":"Sikh diet","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Religious cuisine or diet","intro":[["Followers of Sikhism do not have a preference for meat  or vegetarian consumption. There are two views on initiated or \"Amritdhari Sikhs\" and meat consumption. \"Amritdhari\" Sikhs (i.e. those that follow the Sikh Rehat Maryada - the Official Sikh Code of Conduct) can eat meat (provided it is not Kutha meat). \"Amritdharis\" that belong to some Sikh sects (e.g. Akhand Kirtani Jatha, Damdami Taksal, Namdhari) are vehemently against the consumption of meat and eggs."],["The Sikh Gurus have indicated their preference for a simple diet, which could include meat or be vegetarian. Guru Nanak said that overconsumption of food i.e. Lobh (Greed) involves a drain on the Earth's resources and thus on life. In the case of meat, passages from the Guru Granth Sahib (the holy book of Sikhs, also known as the Adi Granth) say that fools argue over this issue. The tenth guru, Guru Gobind Singh, prohibited the Sikhs from the consumption of Kutha (any ritually slaughtered) meat because of the Sikh belief that sacrificing an animal in the name of God is mere ritualism (something to be avoided)."],["In Sikhism, only lacto-vegetarian food is served in the Gurdwara (Sikh temple) but Sikhs are not bound to be meat-free. The general consensus is that Sikhs are free to choose whether to adopt a meat diet or not."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diet_in_Sikhism"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Menus and Memories from Punjab: Meals to Nourish Body and Soul","ref":"cookbook/menus-and-memories-from-punjab-meals-to-nourish-body-and-soul","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Veronica Sidhu","note":"Punjabi family cookbook and memoir from a New Jersey Sikh household, with a glossary of food and religious terms.","url":"","suggested":true}]}],"cont":null},"cuisine/american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/american-cuisine","n":"American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of United States of America","intro":[["American cuisine encompasses the cooking style and range of dishes prepared in the United States. It primarily derives from a mixing of European cuisine, Native American and Alaskan cuisine, and African American cuisine known as soul food. The Northeast, Midwest, Mid-Atlantic, South, West, Southwest, and insular areas all have distinctive elements, reflecting native food resources, local demographics, and culinary innovation. These developments have also given some states and cities distinctive elements. Hawaiian cuisine reflects substantial influence from its native Polynesian cuisine and from East Asian cuisine, while Louisiana Creole integrates elements from French, West African, and Native American cuisines. Proximity and territorial expansion have also led to substantial influence from Latin American cuisine, including new forms such as Tex-Mex and New Mexican cuisine. Native American cuisines such as Tlingit cuisine have persisted."],["Modern mass media and global immigration have brought influences from many other cultures, and some elements of American food culture have become global exports. Some traditions are regional heritages associated with a particular ethnic group, including Cajun and Pennsylvania Dutch. Several states have unique cuisines, such as Minnesotan cuisine and California cuisine. Some immigrant groups have had their cuisines evolve, resulting in a distinct tradition, such as Chinese American, German American, Irish American, Italian American, Greek American, Jewish American and Mexican American."],["American cooking dates back to the traditions of the Native Americans, whose diet included a mix of farmed and hunted food, and varied widely across the continent. The Colonial period created a mix of New World and Old World cookery and brought new crops and livestock. During the early 19th century, cooking was based mostly on what the agrarian population could grow, hunt, or raise on their land. With an increasing influx of immigrants and a move to city life, American food further diversified in the latter part of the 19th century. The 20th century saw a revolution in cooking as new technologies, the World Wars, a scientific understanding of food, and continued immigration combined to create a wide range of new foods. This has allowed for the rich diversity of dishes throughout the country. The popularity of the automobile in the 20th century also influenced American eating habits in the form of drive-in and drive-through restaurants."],["American cuisine includes milkshakes, barbecue, and a wide range of fried foods. Many quintessential American dishes are unique takes on foods originally from other culinary traditions, including burgers, pizza, hot dogs, and Tex-Mex. Regional cooking includes a range of fish dishes in the coastal states, gumbo, and various sandwiches i.e. Peanut Butter Jelly, cheesesteak. American cuisine has specific foods that are eaten on holidays, such as a turkey at Thanksgiving dinner or Christmas dinner. Modern American cuisine includes a focus on fast food and take-out food, often of ethnic origin. The culinary scene in the country is shaped by televised celebrity chefs, social media, and foodie culture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Taste of Home All-American Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/taste-of-home-all-american-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Taste of Home","note":"Classic home recipes organized by US region.","url":"https://simonandschuster.ca/books/Taste-of-Home-All-American-Cookbook/Taste-of-Home/9781621459279","suggested":true},{"name":"The Cook's Country Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-cook-s-country-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Cook's Country (America's Test Kitchen)","note":"Tested versions of classic, regional, and heirloom American home recipes.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":146,"links":[["5-8 Club","dish/5-8-club"],["Altabira City Tavern","dish/altabira-city-tavern"],["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Applebee's","dish/applebee-s"],["Arby's","dish/arby-s"],["Arnold's Bar and Grill","dish/arnold-s-bar-and-grill"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Ben's Chili Bowl","dish/ben-s-chili-bowl"],["Benjy's","dish/benjy-s"],["Besaw's","dish/besaw-s"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blackhawk (restaurant)","dish/blackhawk-restaurant"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Bobcat Bite","dish/bobcat-bite"],["Boiling Springs Tavern","dish/boiling-springs-tavern"],["Brix Tavern","dish/brix-tavern"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brown Derby","dish/brown-derby"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo